# welcome.view, the package's health check and its smallest showcase.
# Lines starting with # never render: this file is the tutorial, and the box
# it draws tells you where to go next.
# Two rules bite when you write your own: directives sit at column 0 (an
# indented directive is plain text), and body whitespace is content, it is
# what keeps the columns below aligned.

# A titled, framed box. @right is a badge SET INTO the top rule, near its right
# corner; @head is the title row, the first line inside the border, above the
# full-width rule. The box sizes itself to its widest line.
# The version is written by scripts/sync-version.mjs at every bump, never by
# hand: a .view cannot import the number, and this line is the only place a
# reader learns WHICH engine drew the box when two of them could have.
@box
@head {{box_title}}Welcome!{{/}}
@right @tayomi/cc-views v1.0.27

# The health-check line is STATIC on purpose: an empty view:welcome block
# renders this box word for word, so the agent has nothing to improvise and
# the human recognises the canonical output at a glance. It stays FULL WIDTH,
# above the two-column zone: the first thing read is never narrowed.
# The backticks are the check, not decoration: the one word they wrap is the
# one word that comes out coloured, so the line proves inline code rendering
# on the very screen it greets you from.
If you can read this with `style`, everything works! 🎉🎉
# @rule draws an inner divider. Keep it glued to the lines around it: the box
# collapses blank runs, and a rule floating between two blanks collapses with
# them. It is full width too, filled to the border, which is exactly why the
# language forbids one INSIDE a region: a border-wide dash across two columns
# would mean nothing.
@rule
# @aside NAMES a view and lays its rows in a second column, on the LEFT of
# every line up to @endaside. The named file is read as plain rows and no
# directive in it is honoured, which is why views/tayo.view ships frameless.
# The column costs its own printed width plus five columns (two spaces, the
# separator, two spaces), and it is dropped WHOLE the moment that would leave
# the main flow under 40 columns of box content: on a narrow terminal you get
# the text, never a ribbon. The two columns centre against each other; add
# `top` or `bottom` after the name to pin them to an edge instead.
@aside tayo
 {{dim}}what you can do now{{/}}

# A section in the TL;DR style: a coloured label, a gutter bar, the text.
# {{tags}} weigh zero columns, so the labels are padded BY HAND to seven
# columns and the bars line up: that is the whitespace-is-content rule at
# work. The bar is U+258E, full height, so consecutive lines join into one
# frange. A blank label continues the section above.
# A blank line inside the region SURVIVES: the composed line still carries
# the art and the separator, so the box has no blank run to collapse.
 {{cyan}}LEARN  {{/}}  {{cyan}}▎{{/}} this very box is a template: read
 {{cyan}}       {{/}}  {{cyan}}▎{{/}} {{code}}views/welcome.view{{/}}, commented line by line

 {{gold}}CREATE {{/}}  {{gold}}▎{{/}} drop {{code}}yours.view{{/}} in your project's {{code}}views/{{/}}
 {{gold}}       {{/}}  {{gold}}▎{{/}} and write {{code}}view:yours{{/}} blocks: same name
 {{gold}}       {{/}}  {{gold}}▎{{/}} shadows any view, even this one

 {{green}}ASK    {{/}}  {{green}}▎{{/}} your agent writes your template for you: the
 {{green}}       {{/}}  {{green}}▎{{/}} {{code}}write-view{{/}} skill ships in the repo below

# This box is deliberately static, but a real view is fed DATA. The worked
# examples do not ship in the package (the tarball carries only dist/ and
# views/), so this pointer goes to the repository instead.
 {{orange}}EXPLORE{{/}}  {{orange}}▎{{/}} this box is all static, but views carry data:
 {{orange}}       {{/}}  {{orange}}▎{{/}} fields, maps, chips, dressed by the template.
 {{orange}}       {{/}}  {{orange}}▎{{/}} worked examples:
 {{orange}}       {{/}}  {{orange}}▎{{/}} https://github.com/mopi1402/tayomi-cc-views
@endaside

# @foot names a FIELD, never text: the bottom zone exists only when the block
# carries `message:`, and vanishes without a trace when it does not. This is
# the conditional the language does not otherwise have, and the one OPTIONAL
# datum of this view: data decides presence, the template decides looks.
# It sits below @endaside, so the zone stays full width like the rule above.
@foot message
@endbox
